Yes. That is simpler—and I think it is the right primitive picture for the game.

The universe begins with a preferred reference geometry:

- orthogonal;
- isometric;
- small;
- with every pair of points possessing a preferred separation.

Call the original distance between points \(p\) and \(q\):

\[
d_0(p,q).
\]

After the universe evolves, their actual separation becomes:

\[
d(p,q,t).
\]

The pairwise strain is simply:

\[
\varepsilon_{pq}
=
\frac{d(p,q,t)-d_0(p,q)}{d_0(p,q)}.
\]

Then give the connection a stretchiness or stiffness \(k_{pq}\). The restoring tension is:

\[
T_{pq}=k_{pq}\,\varepsilon_{pq}.
\]

So the universe is effectively an elastic web of preferred distance relations. Dynamics move points away from their original relational geometry; the resulting mismatch generates tension.

The one distinction I would preserve is:

> The tension is not itself the metric. The metric is the current system of distances; tension is the universe’s response to the difference between the current metric and its preferred metric.

So:

\[
\text{reference metric}
\longrightarrow
\text{displacement}
\longrightarrow
\text{metric distortion}
\longrightarrow
\text{tension}.
\]

And then the tension affects subsequent motion:

\[
\text{tension}
\longrightarrow
\text{new displacement}.
\]

That gives a closed feedback loop.

The elastic energy of the whole universe could be written in the simplest discrete form as:

\[
E=
\frac12
\sum_{p<q}
k_{pq}
\left[d(p,q)-d_0(p,q)\right]^2.
\]

The universe “wants” to minimize that energy, meaning it tends to recover its original orthogonal, isometric relationships.

This also makes the primitive dynamics much easier to understand.

An axis expansion moves points apart preferentially along one direction. A plane rotation moves points according to a rotational displacement field. Multiple dynamics simply add their attempted displacements:

\[
u(p)=u_x(p)+u_{xy}(p)+u_{zw}(p)+\cdots
\]

The pairwise distance network then determines whether that combined motion is harmless or distortive.

A global rigid rotation changes no pairwise distances:

\[
d(p,q,t)=d_0(p,q),
\]

so it creates no tension. That is exactly what we want.

But differential rotation—one region rotating faster than another—changes pairwise distances and therefore creates shear tension.

Likewise:

- uniform translation creates no tension;
- rigid rotation creates no tension;
- uniform isotropic scaling creates uniform strain;
- anisotropic expansion creates directional tension;
- opposing expansion and contraction create strong strain;
- relative rotation across a shared surface creates torsion and shear.

This means we may not even need to arbitrarily declare many axis–axis, plane–plane, or axis–plane couplings.

They become coupled automatically whenever their displacement fields affect the same point relationships.

For example, suppose \(x\)-expansion and \(xy\)-rotation are both active. They do not need a special symbolic interaction law. Both move points. Their combined motion changes the same collection of pairwise distances, and the elastic network generates the interaction between them.

So coupling can now mean:

> Two dynamics are coupled when their displacement fields overlap within the same tension-bearing distance network.

That naturally permits:

- axis–axis coupling;
- plane–plane coupling;
- axis–plane coupling;
- cross-shell coupling;
- multi-dynamic coupling.

No category problem remains, because axes and planes are not directly pulling on one another. They are both deforming the same relational geometry.

For the split sphere, the northern and southern hemispheres share points—or shared distance relations—across the equatorial contact plane. Initially that plane has a flat metric. If the north and south evolve identically, no mismatch develops. If they evolve differently, the common network cannot satisfy both preferred motions simultaneously, so tension accumulates along the interface.

That tension can:

- stretch the interface;
- shear it;
- bend the surrounding geometry;
- transmit motion from one hemisphere to the other;
- or produce a stable compromise configuration.

The cleanest toy rule is therefore:

\[
\boxed{
\text{Every pair of points remembers a preferred distance.}
}
\]

\[
\boxed{
\text{Dynamics displace points.}
}
\]

\[
\boxed{
\text{Departures from preferred distances create tension.}
}
\]

\[
\boxed{
\text{The evolving collection of actual distances is the emergent metric.}
}
\]

That is essentially an elastic relational universe. The metric is not imposed as a separate field; it is the instantaneous record of how the original isometric network has been stretched, compressed, and sheared.

For the toy version we are describing, it is quite calculable. The difficulty depends mostly on how many points we use and whether every point is connected to every other point.

The simplest version is essentially an elastic network.

Start with points \(X_i\) in the original small orthogonal universe. Each connected pair remembers its original distance:

\[
L_{ij}^{0}=\lVert X_j-X_i\rVert.
\]

The active axis expansions and plane rotations propose new positions:

\[
x_i^{\text{attempted}}
=
\Phi_{\text{dynamics}}(X_i).
\]

The actual distance becomes:

\[
L_{ij}=\lVert x_j-x_i\rVert,
\]

and the strain of that connection is:

\[
\varepsilon_{ij}
=
\frac{L_{ij}-L_{ij}^{0}}{L_{ij}^{0}}.
\]

With stiffness \(k_{ij}\), its tension is approximately:

\[
T_{ij}=k_{ij}\varepsilon_{ij}.
\]

The corresponding elastic energy is:

\[
E_{ij}
=
\frac12 k_{ij}
\left(L_{ij}-L_{ij}^{0}\right)^2.
\]

Add every connection:

\[
E_{\text{total}}
=
\sum_{i<j}E_{ij}.
\]

The resulting shape is the configuration of points that minimizes this total energy while also being driven by the imposed dynamics.

So computationally, we ask:

> Where can all the points move so that the universe comes as close as possible to satisfying the competing expansions, contractions, and rotations while minimizing accumulated tension?

That is a standard numerical optimization problem.

## Three levels of difficulty

A small illustrative model is easy.

A sphere represented by perhaps 100–1,000 points, each connected only to nearby neighbors, could be evolved comfortably in Python. We could calculate:

- the deformed shape;
- every connection’s tension;
- local compression and extension;
- principal strain directions;
- total stored energy;
- where the geometry buckles or concentrates strain.

This is probably a few hundred lines of code, not a research-scale solver.

A denser, smooth model is moderate.

For perhaps 10,000–100,000 points, we would use a tetrahedral mesh and finite-element-like calculations. That would allow smooth surfaces, interior strain, and better curvature estimates. It is still practical, but mesh quality and numerical stability start mattering.

A physically serious spacetime model is hard.

That would require deciding:

- the true constitutive law;
- how tension propagates;
- whether the material is elastic, plastic, viscoelastic, or something stranger;
- whether connections are local or nonlocal;
- how expansion and rotation rates change in response to tension;
- whether information propagates at a finite characteristic speed;
- and how the resulting metric affects subsequent dynamics.

But we do not need any of that to begin exploring shapes.

## The important computational choice

Connecting every point to every other point grows as:

\[
\frac{N(N-1)}2.
\]

For \(N=10{,}000\), that is almost fifty million connections. This becomes expensive and also creates a highly nonlocal, extremely rigid universe.

Instead, we would usually connect each point to nearby neighbors. Then the original geometry behaves like an elastic continuum.

A useful compromise is:

- strong local connections;
- weaker long-range connections;
- special strong connections across declared \(HH\) interfaces.

That preserves the idea that all points participate in one preferred geometry without requiring equally strong direct springs between every pair.

## How an axis dynamic becomes a displacement

For expansion along \(x\) with rate \(h_x\):

\[
\dot x=h_xx.
\]

Over a short time step \(\Delta t\):

\[
x' \approx x(1+h_x\Delta t).
\]

Contraction is simply \(h_x<0\).

For anisotropic dynamics:

\[
\begin{pmatrix}
x'\\y'\\z'\\w'
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
e^{h_x\Delta t}&0&0&0\\
0&e^{h_y\Delta t}&0&0\\
0&0&e^{h_z\Delta t}&0\\
0&0&0&e^{h_w\Delta t}
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
x\\y\\z\\w
\end{pmatrix}.
\]

This provides the attempted motion before elastic resistance is applied.

## How a plane rotation becomes a displacement

For an \(xy\) rotation:

\[
\begin{pmatrix}
x'\\y'
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos\theta&-\sin\theta\\
\sin\theta&\cos\theta
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
x\\y
\end{pmatrix}.
\]

A rigid rotation of the entire sphere leaves all distances unchanged, so it creates no strain.

But any of these does create strain:

- one hemisphere rotates and the other does not;
- rotation rate varies with radius;
- two regions rotate in opposite directions;
- the interface resists sliding;
- rotation is combined with anisotropic expansion.

That distinction will emerge automatically from the distance calculations.

## The actual equilibrium calculation

We would not merely apply a transformation and stop. We would alternate two processes.

First, dynamics propose displacement:

\[
x_i\rightarrow x_i+\Delta x_i^{\text{dynamic}}.
\]

Then elastic tension pulls the points toward a lower-energy configuration:

\[
x_i\rightarrow x_i-\eta\nabla_{x_i}E_{\text{total}},
\]

where \(\eta\) is a small numerical step.

Repeated over time:

\[
\text{dynamic forcing}
\rightarrow
\text{strain}
\rightarrow
\text{restoring motion}
\rightarrow
\text{new shape}.
\]

This would allow the geometry to buckle, stretch, twist, and settle rather than merely displaying the raw transformation.

## What “shape” means in four dimensions

For a 3D sphere model, we can visualize the result directly.

For a four-dimensional \(H\), we would compute the deformation in \(x,y,z,w\), but display it through:

- 3D slices at selected \(w\);
- 3D projections;
- principal strain ellipsoids;
- color maps of tension;
- animated trajectories through \(w\) or time;
- interface surfaces between paired \(H\) structures.

The calculation in four dimensions is not dramatically harder than in three dimensions. Visualization is the harder part.

## What we could calculate for each random universe

For every rolled universe, the solver could report:

\[
\operatorname{tr}\varepsilon
\]

for local volume expansion or contraction,

\[
