## Practical verdict

A first working solver is not especially difficult.

The sensible first model would be:

- one 3D sphere;
- perhaps 500–2,000 points;
- local elastic connections;
- a northern and southern hemisphere;
- a shared equatorial interface;
- independently assigned axis and rotation dynamics;
- one stretchiness parameter;
- iterative energy minimization;
- a 3D plot colored by tension.

That would be enough to see whether the idea produces intuitive forms: ellipsoids, saddles, twisted waists, pinches, lobes, shear bands, and buckled interfaces.

The genuinely difficult part is not calculating a shape. It is deciding which elastic rules deserve to be interpreted as physics rather than as one interesting toy material among many.

