\text{possible kinematic universes}
\]

Version two should generate:

\[
\text{possible kinematic universes}
+
\text{possible laws governing them}.
\]

That means the program can still be wildly inventive, but every universe comes with enough rules to make its weirdness traceable.

The important distinction is not:

\[
\text{right interpretation}
\quad\text{vs.}\quad
\text{wrong interpretation}.
\]

It is:

\[
\text{declared rule}
\quad\text{vs.}\quad
\text{undeclared assumption}.
\]

An undeclared assumption is not necessarily bad during exploration. It is often how the interesting rules are discovered. Once one becomes compelling, we promote it into the formal game.

So I would frame the progression like this:

**Version 1: Kinematic Spree**

- roll axes;
- roll rotational planes;
- roll rates;
- roll H/HH structures;
- roll loosely defined couplings;
- explore interpretations afterward.

**Version 2: Physics Spree**

- roll the same kinematics;
- roll or select what rotation means;
- roll what coupling preserves;
- roll what mismatch produces;
- roll whether the substrate is elastic, holonomic, micropolar, wavefront-driven, and so on;
- allow added objects only after generating their properties;
- calculate consequences from that declared rule set.

And we should keep the first version’s freedom. The program should not become a cautious validator that refuses to say anything interesting. It should become a machine capable of saying:

> In this universe, under these invented laws, this particular combination produces a screw field, a metric knot, a rigid inclusion, an induction vortex, or a stable HH counterrotation—and here is exactly why.

That is the interesting upgrade: not fewer speculative conclusions, but **better-defined speculative conclusions**.
